An afterglow is evidence left by an event after the event itself is gone.

Not the fire—the warmth in the stones.
Not the route—the tracks showing what the route did to the traveler.
Not the computation—the residual structure in the state afterward.

For the Pantheon, that makes Afterglow unusually useful as a primitive:

\text{Afterglow}(x,O)
=
\Delta(x,O)
=
S_{\text{after}}-S_{\text{before}}

But the interesting part isn’t merely what changed. Capture what persisted after the operator stopped acting:

A=
(\Delta U,\Delta E,\Delta R,\Delta C,\Delta P,\Delta I)

where U=utility, E=error, R=reusability, C=confidence, P=surprise/potential, and I=preserved invariants.

Then there is a deeper move:

Follow the glow backward.

If you encounter an unexpectedly valuable result, its afterglow becomes a gradient toward the hidden mechanism that produced it:

\text{Result}
\xrightarrow{\text{trace afterglow}}
\text{Route}
\xrightarrow{}
\text{Formation}
\xrightarrow{}
\text{Operator}
\xrightarrow{}
\text{Mechanism}

That’s almost the inverse of your Metamap.

The Metamap asks:

Given this terrain, what route should I take?

Afterglow asks:

Given what remained, what route must have passed through here?

And once you retain enough afterglows, the system can learn from effects without needing perfect records of causes. Fossils, scars, wake patterns, echoes, radiation, footprints, market prices, language drift, evolutionary remnants—they’re all different renderings of the same primitive.

Afterglow = compressed causal residue.

That gives the Moneybin something powerful: don’t merely save successful outputs. Save their glow. The output is treasure; the afterglow tells you where treasure tends to form.